  "mainEntity": [
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "What makes an adhesive pressure-sensitive?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "A pressure-sensitive adhesive bonds to substrates under light, finger-pressure alone — without heat, solvent, or chemical activation. The defining property is viscoelasticity: the PSA must be soft enough to flow into and wet the substrate surface under light pressure (liquid-like behaviour), yet elastic enough to resist peel forces when the bond is challenged (solid-like behaviour). The Dahlquist criterion quantitatively defines this balance: a material is pressure-sensitive when its storage modulus G' at 1 Hz is below approximately 100 kPa at the use temperature."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "What is the PSA performance triangle of tack, peel, and shear?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "PSA performance is governed by three interdependent properties that exist in fundamental tension with each other. Tack (or quick-stick) is the immediate bonding force when the adhesive contacts a substrate under light pressure. Peel adhesion is the force required to remove the PSA tape from a surface at a defined angle. Shear strength (or cohesive strength) is the resistance to sustained load applied parallel to the bond plane. Increasing tack typically requires a lower modulus, which reduces shear resistance. Maximising shear via crosslinking reduces tack. The art of PSA formulation is optimising the balance of these three properties for the specific end application."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "How does monomer selection control acrylic PSA properties?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "Acrylic PSA performance is engineered through the glass transition temperature (Tg) of the copolymer, which is set by the ratio and type of monomers used. Soft monomers with very low Tg — such as 2-ethylhexyl acrylate (Tg = -70°C) and n-butyl acrylate (Tg = -54°C) — provide flexibility, flow, and tack. Hard monomers with high Tg — such as methyl methacrylate (Tg = +105°C) and vinyl acetate — provide cohesive strength. Functional monomers such as acrylic acid and hydroxyethyl acrylate introduce carboxyl or hydroxyl groups that serve as internal crosslink sites and provide polar adhesion to difficult substrates like metals and glass."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "Why are tackifier resins added to rubber-based PSA formulations?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "Natural rubber and SIS/SBS block copolymers in their pure form have a storage modulus that is too high for adequate pressure-sensitive tack — they are too elastic and will not flow sufficiently to wet a substrate under light pressure. Tackifier resins (hydrocarbon resins, rosin esters, terpene resins) are added to selectively plasticise the soft rubber midblock of the polymer, lowering its Tg and modulus into the pressure-sensitive range. The tackifier also increases the surface energy of the adhesive, improving wetting on low-energy substrates. The resin-to-rubber ratio is the primary lever for adjusting the balance of tack, peel, and shear in rubber PSA formulations."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "In what applications does rubber PSA outperform acrylic PSA?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "Rubber-based PSAs, particularly SIS/SBS hot-melt formulations, outperform acrylics in applications requiring very high initial quick-stick tack on low-energy surfaces such as polyethylene, polypropylene, and waxed cardboard — where the high-tack tackified rubber system wets the surface immediately. They are also preferred in high-speed label application where fast bonding at the point of dispensing is critical. General packaging tape, masking tapes, and repositionable labels typically use rubber PSA. However, rubber PSAs are significantly inferior to acrylics in UV resistance, high-temperature performance, and long-term aging on outdoor or exposed surfaces."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "How is peel adhesion measured and what standards apply?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "Peel adhesion is the force per unit width required to remove a PSA tape from a standard test panel (typically stainless steel or glass) at a defined peel angle — most commonly 90° or 180°. It is expressed in N/25 mm or oz/inch. The primary test standard is ASTM D3330 (Standard Test Method for Peel Adhesion of Pressure-Sensitive Tape), which covers 180° peel as well as peel adhesion at various dwell times. The peel test separates adhesive failure (at the tape-substrate interface, cohesive failure (within the adhesive layer), and substrate failure, each of which provides different diagnostic information about the PSA formulation."
      }
    }
  ]
};

