      <!-- SECTION 6 -->
      <section class="section" id="section-6">
        <h2>6. Coating Methods, Converting &amp; Quality Testing</h2>
        <p>
          Translating a PSA formulation from the laboratory into a commercially coated tape or label product requires mastery of continuous coating and converting processes, each of which introduces its own process-parameter sensitivities that directly affect the adhesive's final performance properties. The coating method determines the thickness uniformity, surface texture, and bond-line integrity of the final PSA product — all of which directly influence the peel and tack values measured in quality control testing.
        </p>

        <h3 style="font-family: 'Outfit', sans-serif; font-size: 1.3rem; font-weight: 700; color: var(--text-primary); margin-top: 24px; margin-bottom: 12px;">A. Coating Methods</h3>
        <ul style="padding-left: 20px; line-height: 1.8; margin-bottom: 20px;">
          <li><strong>Knife-Over-Roll (Direct Gravure):</strong> A doctor blade or knife sets the adhesive film thickness by controlling the gap between the knife and the substrate. Simple and accurate for moderate-viscosity coatings. Widely used for emulsion acrylic PSA.</li>
          <li><strong>Reverse Roll Coating:</strong> Three-roll system where the applicator roll rotates counter to the substrate travel direction. Excellent for applying uniform thin films of lower-viscosity acrylic emulsions and solvent-based PSAs.</li>
          <li><strong>Slot-Die (Extrusion Die) Coating:</strong> Adhesive is pumped through a precision slot die directly onto the moving substrate. Zero contact pressure, excellent coat weight uniformity at high line speeds. Dominant for both hot-melt rubber PSA and high-end acrylic PSA applications.</li>
          <li><strong>Curtain Coating:</strong> Adhesive falls as a free-falling curtain onto the substrate below. Enables very high line speeds and contactless coating — preferred for delicate film substrates prone to deformation.</li>
        </ul>

        <h3 style="font-family: 'Outfit', sans-serif; font-size: 1.3rem; font-weight: 700; color: var(--text-primary); margin-top: 24px; margin-bottom: 12px;">B. Quality Testing Protocol</h3>
        <p>
          A standard PSA quality control programme covers the full tack-peel-shear performance triangle and adds ancillary tests for liner release force, coat weight, and aging performance. Teams developing PSA products for the first time benefit significantly from the formulation development and commercialisation pathway described in our guide on <a href="/from-lab-to-market" target="_top" style="color: var(--primary); text-decoration: underline; font-weight: 600;">taking a chemical formulation from lab to commercial launch</a>.
        </p>
        <ul style="padding-left: 20px; line-height: 1.8; margin-bottom: 20px;">
          <li><strong>Loop Tack / Probe Tack (Initial Bond):</strong> PSTC-16 (loop tack) or custom probe tack instrument. Measures quick-stick under controlled contact force and time. Key specification for label applications where machine-speed bonding must occur on first contact.</li>
          <li><strong>Peel Adhesion at 90° and 180° (ASTM D3330):</strong> Tape applied to standard stainless steel panel, dwelled for defined period (20 min, 24 hr), then peeled at 300 mm/min. Reported in N/25 mm. Failure mode noted (adhesive, cohesive, or substrate).</li>
          <li><strong>Shear Adhesion / Holding Power (ASTM D3654):</strong> Tape bonded to panel at defined area, loaded with a standard weight (500 g or 1 kg) parallel to the bond plane. Time-to-failure reported in minutes or hours. Accelerated at 70°C for heat resistance grades.</li>
          <li><strong>Coat Weight (g/m²):</strong> Gravimetric measurement on a known area. Critical for batch-to-batch consistency and cost control — even ±1 g/m² variation in acrylic emulsion coat weight translates directly to measurable peel force variation.</li>
          <li><strong>Aging Tests:</strong> Accelerated UV aging (Xenon arc weatherometer per ASTM G155), heat aging (70°C oven), and humidity cycling (50°C/90% RH) — essential for outdoor, medical, and food-contact PSA qualification.</li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <!-- FAQ SECTION -->
      <section class="section" id="faq" style="border-top: 2px solid var(--border); padding-top: 40px; margin-top: 40px;">
        <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
        <div style="display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 20px; margin-top: 20px;">

          <div class="box" style="margin: 0; background: #ffffff; border: 1px solid var(--border); padding: 20px; border-radius: var(--radius-md);">
            <h3 style="color: var(--primary); font-size: 1.15rem; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px;">1. What makes an adhesive pressure-sensitive?</h3>
            <p style="margin: 0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 0.95rem;">
              A pressure-sensitive adhesive bonds to substrates under light finger-pressure alone — without heat, solvent, or chemical activation. The defining property is viscoelasticity: the PSA must be soft enough to flow into and wet the substrate surface under light pressure (liquid-like behaviour), yet elastic enough to resist peel forces when the bond is challenged (solid-like behaviour). The Dahlquist criterion defines this balance: a material is pressure-sensitive when its storage modulus G' at 1 Hz is below approximately 100 kPa at the use temperature.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="box" style="margin: 0; background: #ffffff; border: 1px solid var(--border); padding: 20px; border-radius: var(--radius-md);">
            <h3 style="color: var(--primary); font-size: 1.15rem; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px;">2. What is the PSA performance triangle of tack, peel, and shear?</h3>
            <p style="margin: 0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 0.95rem;">
              PSA performance is governed by three interdependent properties in fundamental tension. Tack (quick-stick) is the immediate bonding force under light pressure. Peel adhesion is the force to remove the tape from a surface at a defined angle. Shear strength is the resistance to sustained parallel load. Increasing tack requires a lower modulus that reduces shear; maximising shear via crosslinking reduces tack. PSA formulation is a balance act within this triangle, calibrated for the specific application.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="box" style="margin: 0; background: #ffffff; border: 1px solid var(--border); padding: 20px; border-radius: var(--radius-md);">
            <h3 style="color: var(--primary); font-size: 1.15rem; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px;">3. How does monomer selection control acrylic PSA properties?</h3>
            <p style="margin: 0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 0.95rem;">
              Acrylic PSA performance is engineered through the copolymer's Tg, set by the ratio and type of monomers. Soft monomers (2-EHA, n-BA) with very low Tg provide flexibility and tack. Hard monomers (MMA, VAc) provide cohesive strength. Functional monomers (acrylic acid, HEA) introduce polar groups for adhesion to metals and glass, and reactive sites for crosslinking. The Fox equation predicts the resulting copolymer Tg from monomer Tgs and weight fractions, making monomer formulation a quantitatively predictable engineering discipline.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="box" style="margin: 0; background: #ffffff; border: 1px solid var(--border); padding: 20px; border-radius: var(--radius-md);">
            <h3 style="color: var(--primary); font-size: 1.15rem; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px;">4. Why are tackifier resins added to rubber-based PSA formulations?</h3>
            <p style="margin: 0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 0.95rem;">
              Natural rubber and SIS/SBS block copolymers in their pure form have a modulus too high for pressure-sensitive tack. Tackifier resins selectively plasticise the soft rubber midblock, lowering its Tg and modulus into the pressure-sensitive range. The tackifier also increases surface energy, improving wetting on low-energy substrates. The resin-to-rubber ratio is the primary lever for adjusting the tack-peel-shear balance in rubber PSA formulations.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="box" style="margin: 0; background: #ffffff; border: 1px solid var(--border); padding: 20px; border-radius: var(--radius-md);">
            <h3 style="color: var(--primary); font-size: 1.15rem; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px;">5. In what applications does rubber PSA outperform acrylic PSA?</h3>
            <p style="margin: 0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 0.95rem;">
              Rubber-based PSAs outperform acrylics in applications requiring very high initial quick-stick tack on low-energy surfaces such as polyethylene, polypropylene, and waxed cardboard — where the tackified rubber system wets the surface immediately. They are preferred in high-speed label application where fast bonding is critical. General packaging tape, masking tapes, and repositionable labels typically use rubber PSA. However, rubber PSAs are significantly inferior to acrylics in UV resistance, high-temperature performance, and long-term aging on outdoor or exposed surfaces.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="box" style="margin: 0; background: #ffffff; border: 1px solid var(--border); padding: 20px; border-radius: var(--radius-md);">
            <h3 style="color: var(--primary); font-size: 1.15rem; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px;">6. How is peel adhesion measured and what standards apply?</h3>
            <p style="margin: 0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 0.95rem;">
              Peel adhesion is the force per unit width required to remove a PSA tape from a standard stainless steel test panel at a defined peel angle (90° or 180°) and speed (typically 300 mm/min), expressed in N/25 mm. The primary test standard is ASTM D3330, which covers both 180° peel and peel at various dwell times. The test distinguishes between adhesive failure (at the tape-substrate interface), cohesive failure (within the adhesive layer), and substrate failure — each providing diagnostic information about the formulation's bond strength and internal integrity.
            </p>
          </div>

        </div>
      </section>

